DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       IInnffVViieeww is a good replacement for the info-stantalone pro-
       gram. The main objetive of InfView is to give to the  user
       a friendly and easy to learn tool to browse the documenta-
       tion found in info format. This format is used by the  FSF
       for all the major tools and of course is used by djgpp.

       This  man page just describes the command line options for
       IInnffVViieeww for more details consult the on-line help.

       The command line options are intentionally similar to  the
       ones provided by iinnffoo((11))..  The first argument, if present,
       is the name of the Info file to read.  Any remaining argu-
       ments  are  treated as the names of menu items in the ini-
       tial node visited. Also note that info files are  searched
       in  the IINNFFOOPPAATTHH directories. To load a file stored in the
       current directory add ./ at the beginning of the name.

       IInnffVViieeww saves the position  of  the  windows,  which  info
       files  are  open, the history of visited nodes, bookmarks,
       etc. in a desktop file. If you invoke  the  program  again
       without  specifying  any info file in the command line the
       desktop file is loaded recreating the last session. If you
       specify  an info file the desktop file won't be loaded. In
       any case the current situation is stored at exit.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       -d, --directory DIR
              Adds a directory to  the  list  of  directories  to
              search. In other words adds DIR to INFOPATH.

       -f, --file FILENAME
              Load  FILENAME  info  file. When you provide a file
              name in the command line only this file is  loaded,
              otherwise  the  program remmembers what file/s were
              opened and opens these files again.

       -k, --use-rh-52-keys
              [[OOnnllyy ffoorr LLiinnuuxx]] Enables the Red Hat 5.2 style key-
              board mapping. That's needed if you are experiment-
              ing some strange function keys behavior. Some  key-
              board mapping files redefines the function key num-
              bers in a good way but really incompatible with the
              old way. I first found it in Red Hat 5.2.

       -l, --force-no-lfn
              [[OOnnllyy  ffoorr  DDOOSS]]  Avoids the use of long file names
              under W9x. Even if  the  LLFFNN  environment  variable
              says the reverse.

       -L, --force-lfn
              [[OOnnllyy  ffoorr  DDOOSS]]  Forces the use of long file names
              under W9x. Even if  the  LLFFNN  environment  variable
              says the reverse.

       -n, --node NODE
              Automatically jumps to this node.

       -r, --read-dkt FILENAME
              If no info file is specified use this desktop file.
              In this way you can retreive the  exact  configura-
              tion  you  were  using  before. It includes: opened
              files, history of visited nodes, bookmarks, etc.

       -s, --save-dkt FILENAME
              Saves the configuration at exit  to  the  specified
              file.  You  can  use  the _-_r option to load it next
              time.

       -h, --help
              Displays the command line options of the editor.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       LLiinnuuxx

       info libc 'function index' printf
              Moves to the node  `Function  Index'  and  then  to
              `printf' in the info file called `libc'.

       DDOOSS

       info libc alpha printf
              Moves  to  the node `Alphabetical list' and then to
              `printf' in the info file called `libc'.

